## Does any load path still write, or read the stale on-disk date?

Enumerated every function that reads whatif.json/scenario files
(grep -n "func.*Load" internal/services/retirement/settings.go):
- `loadInternalContext` — the ONLY write-on-load path (`if changed {
  sm.saveInternal(...) }`); `changed` no longer fires for the bare absent-key
  case, confirmed above.
- `LoadContext`/`LoadContextWithRevision` — cache-hit path calls
  `cloneForCurrentMonth` (prepare.Clone + resolveCurrentMonth on the CLONE
  only; `sm.cache` itself is never mutated or written), so a long-running
  process serves the current month across a cache hit without ever writing
  disk. This clone-on-read mechanism is unchanged from prior attempts (not
  new to attempt 4) but re-verified present and correct.
- `LoadScenarioSettings` — explicit code comment: "intentionally read-only
  — chained-scenario loading during analysis must not rewrite unrelated
  scenario files as a side effect"; calls decodeSettings and discards the
  `changed` flag. Never writes. Confirmed unchanged.
- `reconcileActiveScenarioLocked` (self-heal on missing active-scenario
  file) only flips in-memory `sm.filename`/`sm.cache`/revision; never writes
  a file.

Grepped every non-test .go file referencing "whatif.json"
(`grep -rln "whatif.json" --include="*.go" internal/ cmd/ | grep -v _test.go`):
only settings.go (the manager itself), handlers.go (a doc comment) and
main.go (a doc comment) — no direct file reads anywhere else.

MCP plan tools (`internal/services/mcpsvc/plan`): no direct StartDate/file
handling outside tests; grepped for "StartDate"/"start_date" — only in
*_test.go fixtures. They route through SettingsManager like every other
consumer (confirmed identically to attempt 3's finding, re-verified this
attempt).

sync.go / verdict.go: `internal/handlers/whatif/sync.go` calls
`retirementMgr.LoadContext`/`LoadContextWithRevision` (resolved, not raw).
`internal/handlers/whatif/verdict.go`'s `BuildVerdict` takes an
already-resolved `*models.WhatIfSettings` as a parameter — it does not load
anything itself, so it cannot see a stale date independently of its caller.

Backups (`internal/services/backup`, `internal/services/mcpsvc/admin/{backup,restore}.go`):
copy/restore raw files wholesale; they do not parse StartDate or compute
ages themselves, so the no-write-on-load change cannot introduce a stale
DISPLAYED date there — the next real Load (via SettingsManager, post
BeginExternalRewrite/InvalidateCache) always resolves it. Pre-existing
design, unaffected by this attempt's diff.

## `use_current_month:false` survives every save path
`grep -n "saveInternal\b" internal/services/retirement/settings.go` shows
every mutating method (UpdateSettingsWithPersons, guardrails saves, etc.,
~25 call sites) funnels through `saveInternalAndBump` → `saveInternal`,
which unconditionally starts with `resolveCurrentMonth(settings,
time.Now())` — a no-op for `UseCurrentMonth=false`. Single chokepoint,
unchanged from attempt 3's already-verified finding; re-confirmed present
this attempt.

